  "interpretation": {
    "observed": "One finite isolated pixel dominates the high-resolution film mean. Its chromatic ratio closely matches the medium laser-like source, and its center ray crosses that source quad. This localizes the spike over the source footprint but does not identify the sampled path or primitive member.",
    "candidateNumericalMechanism": "Inherited sphere branch forms b*b-c and roots in float32, uses omega=(x-p)/sampled_radius, and passes it to cos^500 emission without a unit-length check or an upper cosine clamp. For a small sphere near a distant camera, cancellation/root-position error can break the sampled-radius versus recovered-distance agreement. MIS cross-evaluation recovers a normalized direction independently. This mismatch can amplify the narrow profile; it is a hypothesis, not an attribution of this exact pixel.",
    "sourceLocations": [
      "src/render/PPIntegrator.cpp:1611 ppEmitterDirWeight assumes normalized light direction; exponent500 has no upper cosine clamp",
      "src/render/PPIntegrator.cpp:2499 ppSampleDirPrim sphere float discriminant/root and sampled-radius direction",
      "src/render/PPIntegrator.cpp:2352 ppDirPrimPdf recomputes actual length/direction",
      "src/render/PPIntegrator.cpp:6364 custom31 uses standalone contribution times MIS weight"
